## ASSEMBLED SECTION



### <img src="https://flagcdn.com/kr.svg" width="32" alt="South Korea" style={{display: 'inline', verticalAlign: 'middle', position: 'relative', top: '-2px', marginRight: '8px', border: '1px solid #d1d5db', borderRadius: '2px'}} />South Korea

Lee Jae-myung's multi-vector hedging strategy continues operational effectiveness with governance strength maintained through institutional accountability processes and expanding diplomatic architecture. Congressional investigation of Korean regulators over Coupang represents the first significant test of alliance commercial foundations managing regulatory sovereignty disputes, while routine NATO engagement and Japan relations continue established frameworks. Economic fundamentals remain stable with technology partnership diversification through Netherlands dialogue. Domestic political polarization persists but does not undermine Lee's institutional control, as martial law accountability proceeds with second cabinet-level conviction demonstrating rule of law restoration. Marines restructuring toward Indo-Pacific operations suggests potential security posture expansion requiring validation.

**Key developments:**

- **Diplomatic:** Five US investment firms are pursuing investor-state arbitration under KORUS FTA over South Korea's regulatory response to Coupang's data breach. House Judiciary Committee issued subpoena for congressional investigation examining whether Korean enforcement actions unfairly target US companies. Dispute adds to broader Korea-US tensions over regulatory sovereignty.
- **Diplomatic:** Lee met NATO Secretary General Mark Rutte emphasizing Euro-Atlantic and Indo-Pacific security linkages and defense industry cooperation. Separately congratulated Japanese PM Takaichi on election victory, expressing hope for continued shuttle diplomacy. Continues established multi-vector engagement pattern.
- **Economic:** First Korea-Netherlands 2+2 Foreign-Trade Ministers dialogue established cooperation framework on economic security, AI, semiconductors, and critical materials. Joint statement adopted covering technology partnership expansion.
- **Institutional:** Foreign Minister Cho Hyun chaired first preparatory meeting for Korea-Central Asia Summit scheduled September 16-17, 2026, involving 35 government agencies. Represents institutional expansion into Central Asian diplomatic engagement.
- **Domestic:** Former Interior Minister Lee Sang-min sentenced to 7 years for martial law role, second cabinet-level conviction after Han Duck-soo's 23 years. Court again characterized martial law as insurrection. Represents ongoing institutional accountability cycle from December 2024 crisis.

<Accordion title="Other Stories">
- **Kim Jong Un's daughter Kim Ju-ae being designated as successor, intelligence service reports** — South Korea's National Intelligence Service reported that Kim Jong Un's daughter Kim Ju-ae has entered the stage of being formally designated as the regime's successor, marking a shift from previous assessments that described her as being 'trained' for succession. *([bbc.com](https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cn0e1g7kwglo))*
- **Bithumb crypto exchange accidentally distributes $40 billion in Bitcoin to customers** — South Korea's Bithumb cryptocurrency exchange mistakenly credited customers with 620,000 bitcoins worth over $40 billion during a promotional event. The exchange recovered 99.7% of the erroneous credits but faces regulatory scrutiny and questions about system security. *([reuters.com](https://reuters.com/sustainability/boards-policy-regulation/south-korea-crypto-exchange-bithumb-says-system-flaws-led-40-billion-error-2026-02-11))*
- **Lee Jae-myung addresses real estate policy criticism and ownership questions** — President Lee Jae-myung defended his administration's real estate policies amid criticism, stating he owns only one home and that policies aim to reclaim 'unfair privileges' from speculation. He faced questions about forcing multiple homeowners to sell properties. *([chosun.com](https://koreaherald.com/article/10676951))*
- **People Power Party leadership crisis and internal conflicts** — The conservative People Power Party faces internal leadership challenges with leader Jang Dong-hyuk under pressure and party members calling for unity amid factional conflicts. The party's fate may hinge on upcoming Seoul and Busan mayoral races. *([upi.com](https://upi.com/Top_News/World-News/2026/02/09/people-power-party-internal-factional-conflict/4471770685075))*
- **Samsung Galaxy S26 series launch preparations and marketing campaign** — Samsung is preparing for the Galaxy S26 series launch with global marketing campaigns, including giant screens in 17 cities worldwide. The phones are expected to launch in India on February 25 with pre-reservations already started. *([hindustantimes.com](https://www.hindustantimes.com/technology/samsung-galaxy-s26-s26-and-s26-ultra-to-launch-in-india-on-february-25-all-details-101770783787607.html))*
- **North Korea opens housing district for families of soldiers killed in Ukraine war** — North Korea completed a housing district in Pyongyang called Saeppyol Street specifically for families of soldiers killed while fighting alongside Russian forces in Ukraine. The development was attended by Kim Jong Un. *([apnews.com](https://apnews.com/article/north-korea-ukraine-war-dead-kim-jong-un-b589285d79f66900fbc30f9243383cfe))*
- **North Korea threatens 'dire consequences' over alleged drone incursions** — North Korea, through Kim Yo-jong's warning, threatened dire consequences if Seoul doesn't stop alleged drone incursions. The threats could derail President Lee Jae-myung's efforts to improve inter-Korean relations. *([scmp.com](https://www.scmp.com/news/asia/east-asia/article/3343409/north-korea-threatens-dire-consequences-if-seoul-doesnt-stop-drone-incursions))*
</Accordion>

